1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,750
When Jamie said, “Friday looks tight,” Alex

2
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:05,270
heard a different sentence: “You’ve let everyone

3
00:00:05,270 --> 00:00:05,790
down.”

4
00:00:07,209 --> 00:00:09,569
At home, he begins a reply, writes

5
00:00:09,569 --> 00:00:12,389
an apology, deletes it, and replaces it

6
00:00:12,389 --> 00:00:13,209
with a longer one.

7
00:00:14,555 --> 00:00:16,115
Perhaps a warmer opening or a more

8
00:00:16,115 --> 00:00:18,815
confident ending will help; soon he is

9
00:00:18,815 --> 00:00:21,035
editing a conversation that hasn’t happened.

10
00:00:22,495 --> 00:00:23,755
The problem isn’t that he has run

11
00:00:23,755 --> 00:00:25,795
out of answers; he can imagine so

12
00:00:25,795 --> 00:00:27,415
many that none feels safe.

13
00:00:28,922 --> 00:00:31,722
Being clever can help generate possibilities, but

14
00:00:31,722 --> 00:00:34,122
it doesn’t turn those possibilities into evidence,

15
00:00:34,542 --> 00:00:36,782
and overthinking is no proof of intelligence.

16
00:00:38,482 --> 00:00:40,102
A mind can make a prediction sound

17
00:00:40,102 --> 00:00:43,802
convincing simply by supplying detail; Alex can

18
00:00:43,802 --> 00:00:45,982
picture the raised eyebrow without having seen

19
00:00:45,982 --> 00:00:46,282
one.

20
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:50,110
His imagination has produced a scene, not

21
00:00:50,110 --> 00:00:52,370
a response from Jamie, so polishing that

22
00:00:52,370 --> 00:00:54,210
scene cannot tell him what she meant.

23
00:00:55,649 --> 00:00:57,769
Next morning, Alex opens the project schedule

24
00:00:57,769 --> 00:01:00,449
and finds one unfinished review, while the

25
00:01:00,449 --> 00:01:02,089
other tasks are where they should be.

26
00:01:03,446 --> 00:01:05,806
That check changes the problem: he now

27
00:01:05,806 --> 00:01:08,086
knows what might delay Friday, instead of

28
00:01:08,086 --> 00:01:10,526
treating the entire project as a disaster.

29
00:01:12,051 --> 00:01:14,011
Then he imagines Jamie’s face when she

30
00:01:14,011 --> 00:01:17,191
reads his reply, changes a word, imagines

31
00:01:17,191 --> 00:01:19,571
another expression, and changes it back.

32
00:01:21,119 --> 00:01:23,379
Nothing new has entered the picture; his

33
00:01:23,379 --> 00:01:26,419
activity has changed, but his information has

34
00:01:26,419 --> 00:01:26,799
not.

35
00:01:28,252 --> 00:01:30,172
Instead of asking whether he has thought

36
00:01:30,172 --> 00:01:32,472
hard enough, he can ask what this

37
00:01:32,472 --> 00:01:34,612
round of thinking has let him learn.

38
00:01:36,077 --> 00:01:38,457
Sometimes another revision really does uncover a

39
00:01:38,457 --> 00:01:40,437
mistake, which is why a useful check

40
00:01:40,437 --> 00:01:42,977
needs a specific question rather than a

41
00:01:42,977 --> 00:01:43,837
ban on editing.

42
00:01:45,352 --> 00:01:47,612
For Alex, the next useful question is

43
00:01:47,612 --> 00:01:49,332
whether Jamie meant this one review or

44
00:01:49,332 --> 00:01:51,692
the whole deadline; the schedule alone cannot

45
00:01:51,692 --> 00:01:52,432
answer that.

46
00:01:53,778 --> 00:01:56,178
Acting quickly is not always wiser, because

47
00:01:56,178 --> 00:01:58,898
some choices really do deserve slow, uncomfortable

48
00:01:59,418 --> 00:01:59,778
investigation.

49
00:02:01,294 --> 00:02:04,254
Before an expensive commitment or a difficult-to-reverse

50
00:02:04,254 --> 00:02:07,534
decision, checking assumptions can save more trouble

51
00:02:07,534 --> 00:02:08,154
than it costs.

52
00:02:09,831 --> 00:02:11,931
The distinction is what the extra time

53
00:02:11,931 --> 00:02:15,051
is doing: checking a fact, comparing a

54
00:02:15,051 --> 00:02:17,491
real option, or seeking a perspective you

55
00:02:17,491 --> 00:02:17,731
lack.

56
00:02:19,138 --> 00:02:20,558
Or perhaps you are asking the same

57
00:02:20,558 --> 00:02:22,418
facts to promise a feeling of certainty

58
00:02:22,418 --> 00:02:23,478
they cannot supply.

59
00:02:25,050 --> 00:02:27,550
Alex needs a stopping condition, not carelessness:

60
00:02:28,230 --> 00:02:31,070
identify the unfinished review, ask what Jamie

61
00:02:31,070 --> 00:02:33,670
meant, and make one specific proposal.

62
00:02:35,223 --> 00:02:37,843
A stopping condition is provisional: if the

63
00:02:37,843 --> 00:02:40,563
review reveals a serious new problem, he

64
00:02:40,563 --> 00:02:42,583
can investigate again with that new evidence.

65
00:02:44,214 --> 00:02:46,154
It marks what this decision needs now,

66
00:02:46,614 --> 00:02:48,614
rather than making him promise never to

67
00:02:48,614 --> 00:02:49,734
reconsider anything.

68
00:02:51,305 --> 00:02:53,165
On his walk, Alex notices that every

69
00:02:53,165 --> 00:02:55,585
draft has a hidden demand: make Jamie

70
00:02:55,585 --> 00:02:57,605
react exactly as he hopes.

71
00:02:59,125 --> 00:03:01,705
No wording can guarantee that, because another

72
00:03:01,705 --> 00:03:04,565
person brings a mood, a history, and

73
00:03:04,565 --> 00:03:06,245
information he does not have.

74
00:03:07,612 --> 00:03:09,692
He can still be honest and considerate;

75
00:03:10,312 --> 00:03:12,632
those are choices about his message, not

76
00:03:12,632 --> 00:03:14,332
guarantees about her response.

77
00:03:15,971 --> 00:03:18,431
He writes, “I may have misread: did

78
00:03:18,431 --> 00:03:21,191
you mean the review?”—a question Jamie can

79
00:03:21,191 --> 00:03:21,951
actually answer.

80
00:03:23,384 --> 00:03:25,484
He presses send while the doubt remains;

81
00:03:26,324 --> 00:03:29,004
this time, sending begins the conversation instead

82
00:03:29,004 --> 00:03:30,424
of pretending to finish it.

83
00:03:31,704 --> 00:03:34,304
He has made the message answerable, not

84
00:03:34,304 --> 00:03:37,504
invulnerable; Jamie could still be busy, distracted,

85
00:03:37,504 --> 00:03:40,124
or unhappy about something he cannot see.

86
00:03:41,597 --> 00:03:43,417
If the reply takes time, he can

87
00:03:43,417 --> 00:03:45,717
let it remain unknown instead of composing

88
00:03:45,717 --> 00:03:47,897
both sides of the conversation himself.

89
00:03:49,498 --> 00:03:52,178
Jamie replies, “Just the review—I thought you

90
00:03:52,178 --> 00:03:53,898
were waiting for me,” and Alex reads

91
00:03:53,898 --> 00:03:54,358
it twice.

92
00:03:55,861 --> 00:03:57,861
He had interpreted her comment as a

93
00:03:57,861 --> 00:04:00,601
judgment, while she had interpreted his silence

94
00:04:00,601 --> 00:04:02,421
as a request for time.

95
00:04:03,876 --> 00:04:06,136
Neither explanation was hidden inside the original

96
00:04:06,136 --> 00:04:09,156
sentence; they needed another exchange to find

97
00:04:09,156 --> 00:04:09,436
out.

98
00:04:10,884 --> 00:04:12,704
They meet at the café and agree

99
00:04:12,704 --> 00:04:14,444
that Alex will send a draft today,

100
00:04:14,444 --> 00:04:16,204
and Jamie will review it tomorrow.

101
00:04:17,749 --> 00:04:19,989
This doesn’t prove every difficult conversation ends

102
00:04:19,989 --> 00:04:22,869
kindly; it shows what this particular exchange

103
00:04:22,869 --> 00:04:26,609
made possible: information that rehearsal couldn’t provide.

104
00:04:28,124 --> 00:04:30,304
If Jamie had disagreed, he would have

105
00:04:30,304 --> 00:04:32,564
had a real disagreement to discuss, which

106
00:04:32,564 --> 00:04:34,964
is different from repeatedly arguing with an

107
00:04:34,964 --> 00:04:35,764
imagined opponent.

108
00:04:37,272 --> 00:04:38,952
An answer need not be comforting to

109
00:04:38,952 --> 00:04:41,132
be useful; it can reveal a limit,

110
00:04:41,592 --> 00:04:43,692
a missing fact, or a choice that

111
00:04:43,692 --> 00:04:44,572
needs more work.

112
00:04:46,017 --> 00:04:48,797
Alex’s habit of imagining consequences hasn’t vanished;

113
00:04:49,777 --> 00:04:51,637
later, it helps him notice that a

114
00:04:51,637 --> 00:04:53,057
page is missing from the draft.

115
00:04:54,616 --> 00:04:56,376
This time there is something concrete to

116
00:04:56,376 --> 00:04:58,396
check, so he adds the page, checks

117
00:04:58,396 --> 00:05:00,696
the attachment, and sends the work they

118
00:05:00,696 --> 00:05:01,236
agreed on.

119
00:05:02,575 --> 00:05:04,995
When another worried prediction appears, he does

120
00:05:04,995 --> 00:05:06,955
not have to defeat it before continuing.

121
00:05:08,414 --> 00:05:09,874
He can ask whether it points to

122
00:05:09,874 --> 00:05:11,854
a task he can perform, a question

123
00:05:11,854 --> 00:05:13,734
he can ask, or an outcome he

124
00:05:13,734 --> 00:05:14,374
cannot control.

125
00:05:15,946 --> 00:05:17,826
Some worries return even after a useful

126
00:05:17,826 --> 00:05:20,346
decision, so the return of discomfort does

127
00:05:20,346 --> 00:05:22,666
not automatically mean the decision was wrong.

128
00:05:24,101 --> 00:05:26,581
Alex can notice the feeling, check whether

129
00:05:26,581 --> 00:05:29,501
anything has actually changed, and choose whether

130
00:05:29,501 --> 00:05:31,921
this moment calls for another task or

131
00:05:31,921 --> 00:05:32,341
a pause.

132
00:05:33,894 --> 00:05:35,954
Thinking becomes useful again when it serves

133
00:05:35,954 --> 00:05:38,834
those distinctions; the goal is not an

134
00:05:38,834 --> 00:05:40,694
empty mind, but a mind that can

135
00:05:40,694 --> 00:05:41,514
finish a job.
